contentText:
It&#8217;s been a steady climb for the international girl group KATSEYE since its debut in 2024. This week, that ascent culminates with the group&#8217;s first-ever No. 1 on the Billboard 200 — with a historic first-week total.

The five-track EP WILD bows atop the chart this week (dated Aug. 29) with 170,000 first-week units — not only marking the career-best performance for KATSEYE, whose 2025 EP Beautiful Chaos entered at No. 4 with 44,000 units, but also setting a new single-week high for an all-female group since the Billboard 200 switched to measuring albums in units in 2015. Meanwhile, the set&#8217;s new single &#8220;Hootie Frutti&#8221; debuts at No. 31 on the Billboard Hot 100, joining previous releases &#8220;Pinky Up&#8221; and &#8220;Animal&#8221; as the set&#8217;s third top 40 hit on the chart.

How did KATSEYE manage such a massive debut? And are the group&#8217;s achievements being overshadowed by news about members going on hiatus? Billboard staffers and contributors discuss these questions and more below.

1. KATSEYE’s  WILD  debuts atop the Billboard 200 with 170,000 first-week units — the best-ever single-week number for an all-female group. On a scale from 1-10, how big a first-week performance is that for the group?

Kyle Denis : A solid 7.5. Such a robust first week is certainly a testament to their pull despite the&nbsp;lineup&nbsp;drama and the wide range of opinions on their music.&nbsp;

Lyndsey Havens : 9 — and I&#8217;m only docking it a point because&nbsp; WILD&nbsp; is an EP, though I&#8217;ve since convinced myself that maybe it&#8217;s actually more impressive to have such a big first week with only five songs. So a 9.5, then. This debut feels especially triumphant given the year that KATSEYE has had; while there have been lows, like Manon announcing her hiatus in February and Sophia announcing hers this month, there have also been countless highs, from a huge Coachella debut to now earning the biggest single week for an all-women group. This feels like a nice feather in the girl group&#8217;s collective cap.&nbsp;

Andrew Unterberger : At least an 8. That first-week number — and particularly the 145,000 in pure sales it includes — suggests that the group has cultivated an extremely devoted fanbase, perhaps one further galvanized by the constant debate surrounding the group and its membership.

Alicia Urrea : I’d definitely give it a 10/10. It’s hard to overstate how significant this is for KATSEYE; a No. 1 album would have been such an amazing achievement alone, but 170,000 units is genuinely such a historic opening. KATSEYE has gone from a No. 119 debut with&nbsp; SIS (Soft Is Strong) &nbsp;to No. 4 with&nbsp; Beautiful Chaos &nbsp;to No. 1 with&nbsp; WILD &nbsp;in just two years, so this feels like a major confirmation that the group&#8217;s fanbase has grown into an incredible force to be reckoned with.&nbsp;

Kristen Wisneski : I would rate it an 8. The girl group is truly a powerhouse, and its last EP,  Beautiful Chaos  (2025), debuted at No. 4 and featured multiple Hot 100 hits. Given that strong chart history, I’m not surprised that  Wild  debuted so successfully. But debuting at No. 1 on the Billboard 200 with 170,000 units sold is a phenomenal achievement and speaks to the dedication and strength of KATSEYE&#8217;s fanbase. Based on the trajectory so far, I’m confident the group will continue to grow and deliver even stronger music in the future.

2. The group’s 2025 set  Beautiful Chaos  bowed at No. 4 with 44,000 units moved — a little more than a quarter of the  WILD  debut number. What do you think the biggest reason is for the dramatically improved performance of this set?

Kyle Denis : They simply raised their profile from&nbsp;virtually every&nbsp;possible angle. Since&nbsp; Beautiful Chaos , the group picked up two Grammy nods and performed at the 2026 ceremony;&nbsp;played major sets at Coachella,&nbsp;Lollapalooza&nbsp;and Governor’s Ball, and won three American Music Awards. And, of course, Manon’s hiatus announcement, which sparked larger discourse about the treatment of Black members in mixed-race girl groups,&nbsp;helped the group land on the radars of those who would never give their music a second thought. When you combine all of this with each of the EP’s pre-release singles — “Pinky Up,” “Animal” and&nbsp;“Hootie Frutti” — spawning individual viral moments on&nbsp;socials, the improvement between&nbsp; Beautiful Chaos &nbsp;and&nbsp; WILD &nbsp;really&nbsp;isn’t&nbsp;that surprising.&nbsp;

Lyndsey Havens : Well, that&#8217;s the thing about having both highs and lows — they generate buzz all the same. I think interest in KATSEYE is reaching an all-time high, whether it be curiosity over the lineup changes and members&#8217; wellbeing or a growing taste for the group&#8217;s brand of high-energy, sometimes sassy and always fierce brand of supercharged pop. Those are really nice ingredients that, when blended together, result in a recipe for the kind of moment they&#8217;re having right now.&nbsp;

Andrew Unterberger : As big as they were a year ago, in the past 12 months they&#8217;ve really become crossover stars — the kind that play the Grammys, score radio hits, go viral almost by default, and generally get themselves into the kind of spaces that most pop groups have been traditionally shut out of the past 10 years. A year ago they were one of pop&#8217;s biggest new cult acts, now that cult has grown so big and expansive they&#8217;re just one of pop&#8217;s biggest new acts, period.

Alicia Urrea : I think the biggest reason is simply the sheer amount of momentum that was built in between the two releases. Beautiful Chaos was already a major step up for them, and it gave them (and EYEKONS) “Gnarly,” “Gabriela,” Grammy nominations and a much larger audience, and they continued building on all that noise with Coachella, “Pinky Up,” “Animal” and a much bigger live profile. By the time&nbsp; WILD &nbsp;arrived, KATSEYE wasn&#8217;t really a new group anymore. There was already an established fanbase waiting for the project.

Kristen Wisneski : I think the main factor was the rollout strategy. As the group continued to release more music, more fans found their way to KATSEYE, as it came off of a huge tour this past fall and was at both the Coachella and Governor&#8217;s Ball festivals this summer, which have only added to the fanbase. With the announcement of the EP during Coachella Weekend 2 and the hype around lead single “Pinky Up,” the hype was built immediately, and helped lead to these dramatically improved numbers.

3. “Hootie Frutti” debuts at No. 31 on the Hot 100, becoming (for now) the group’s sixth straight hit to peak in the 20s or 30s on the Hot 100. What do you think it will take for the group to reach the top 20 for the first time — or do you think “Hootie” will grow enough to get there itself?

Kyle Denis : I see it going one of two ways. Either the group continues honing this hyperpop girl group niche with more songs like “Hootie” and “Gnarly” — and one of those ends up breaking the top 20 — or&nbsp;they deliver something closer to traditional girl group fare (like “Gabriela”) that feels truly singular to their brand identity.&nbsp;Overall,&nbsp;I do think&nbsp;that&nbsp;the conversation around them&nbsp;has to&nbsp;remain focused on the songs themselves for them to reach the top 20 or higher;&nbsp;there’s&nbsp;still far too much focus on the machinations of their label and their ever-evolving lineup.&nbsp;

Lyndsey Havens : Release “I Won&#8217;t Say (I&#8217;m in Love)”! KATSEYE performed the&nbsp; Hercules&nbsp; classic at this year&#8217;s Disney Legends Ceremony to honor Susan Egan (who originally voiced Megara in the animated film) and it really highlighted the vocal strength of this group. &#8220;Hootie Frutti&#8221; and most other KATSEYE hits are undeniably fun and catchy, but I think if they drop a banger of a ballad — either this cover or an original in sim…
